      <div><head>Introduction</head><p>Spillover is frequent <ref type="bibr" target="#b0">[1]</ref>, <ref type="bibr" target="#b1">[2]</ref>. Surveys agree <ref type="bibr" target="#b0">[1]</ref>-<ref type="bibr" target="#b2">[3]</ref>, as does <ref type="bibr" target="#b1">Smith et al. (2019)</ref>.</p></div>
      <div><head>Methods</head></div>
      <div><head>Trapping</head><p>Traps were set every night.</p></div>
      <figure xml:id="fig_0"><head>Fig. 1 .</head><label>1</label><figDesc>Fig. 1. Trapping sites in the valley <ref type="bibr" target="#b2">[3]</ref>.</figDesc></figure>
      <div><p>Traps were checked at dawn.</p></div>
      <div><head>214</head><p>Rodents were identified by sequencing.</p></div>
      <div><head>Downloaded from https://example.org on 1 May 2021</head><p>Sequences were deposited.</p></div>
      <div><head>Discussion</head><p>Density predicts spillover <ref type="bibr">[9]</ref>.</p></div>
      <div><head>Acknowledgements</head><p>We thank the field team.</p></div>
      <div><p>And the funders.</p></div>
      <figure xml:id="fig_1"><figDesc>Reprints or correspondence: Dr A Smith, a made-up address.</figDesc></figure>
      <figure type="table" xml:id="tab_0"><head>Table 1</head><label>1</label><figDesc>Rodents trapped per season and site.</figDesc></figure>
